ld be J2 or J3, or maybe another tile.\n\nBut without the missing tiles, it's hard. So perhaps the best approach is to discard a tile that is not part of any potential meld. For example, W7 is a single. If I discard it, maybe I can focus on forming chows in the W suit if possible. Alternatively, B1 is a single, but maybe it's better to keep it if it can be part of a pair.\n\nAnother angle: considering the seat wind is West (F4). If I keep F4, maybe I can form a pung later if I draw another F4. Since the current unshown tiles have 4 F4s, but one is in my hand, so there are 3 left. The chance of drawing another F4 is possible. If I form a pung of F4, that would give me 2 points for the seat wind pung. Similarly, if I can form a pung of J2 or J3, that would be a dragon pung (2 points each). But since I have only one of each, it's not possible now.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can aim for a concealed hand (fully concealed, 4 points) if I don't have any exposed melds. Since my melds are empty, if I keep all concealed, that's a 4-point bonus. But to do that, I need to avoid discarding tiles that others might need. For example, if I discard T3, someone might need it for a chow. But the player across played T8, and the previous played T1, so maybe T3 is not in high demand.\n\nLooking at the valid actions: the options are to play T3, W5, J2, W7, T7, W4, T5, T4, B5, B6, J3, B3, F4, B1.\n\nWhich of these is the least useful? Let's see:\n\n- T3 is part of a chow (T3, T4, T5). If I discard T3, I lose that chow. But if I keep it, I have that chow.\n- W5 is part of a possible chow with W4 (if I get W3 or W6). So keeping W5 is better.\n- J2 and J3 are single dragons. Discarding them might not be ideal if they can form a pair or pung later.\n- W7 is a single. If I discard it, maybe I can focus on other chows.\n- T7 is a single. Similarly.\n- F4 is my seat wind. Keeping it is better for potential pung.\n- B1 is a single. If I discard it, maybe someone needs it for a chow (B1, B2, B3), b