re empty, if I discard W7, someone might chi it if they have W6 and W8? Wait, chi requires a sequence. If someone has W6 and W8, they can't chi W7. So W7 is safe to discard.\n\nSimilarly, T7 is part of T7-8-9, but T8 is already played. So T7 might be safe to discard.\n\nBetween W7 and T7, which is better? Let's think about the potential for forming more chows. If I discard W7, I can still work on forming W4-5-6 chow if I get W6. If I discard T7, I can work on T7-8-9, but T8 is already out. So maybe discarding T7 is better because it's less likely to be needed.\n\nAlternatively, maybe keeping T7 for a pair. But I only have one T7. So no.\n\nSo the best move is to discard either W7 or T7. Looking at the valid actions, both are options. Let's check which one is in the valid actions. The user's valid actions include Play W7 and Play T7.\n\nNow, which one to choose? Let's see if there's any other pattern I can aim for. For example, if I can form a concealed hand (4 points) by discarding a tile that's not part of any meld. Both W7 and T7 are singles. So discarding either would help.\n\nAnother angle: the hand has F4 (West Wind) which is my seat wind. If I can form a pung of F4, that's 2 points. Since I have one, maybe keeping it is better. So I shouldn't discard F4. So F4 is safe to keep.\n\nLooking at the other options, between W7 and T7, which is better. Let's see the unshown tiles: T7 has 4 left, W7 has 4 left. So both are equally available. \n\nPerhaps I should discard the tile that is less likely to be needed by others. Since the previous player played T1 and the player across played T8, maybe T7 is less needed. So discarding T7 might be safer. Alternatively, W7 is a character tile, which might be more commonly needed for chows. But without knowing others' hands, it's hard to say.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can look for possible pairs in the hand. For example, if I can form a pair, which would be the final pair in the winning hand. Let's see:\n\n- J2 and J3 are both singles. If I discard one, I can have a pair if I draw another. For example, if I discard J2, then if I draw another J2, I have a pair. Similarly for J3. But I have to discard one of them now. However, the valid actions include Play J2 and Play J3. If I discard J2, maybe I can aim for a pair of J3. But I only have one J3. Similarly for J2. \n\nBut if I discard J2, I lose the chance to form a pung of J2. However, since I have only one, it's not possible. So maybe it's better to keep both J2 and J3 in case they can form a pair. \n\nSo, considering all, the best options are to discard W7 or T7. Between them, perhaps T7 is better because the T8 is already played, making T7 less likely to be needed for a chow. So I'll choose to discard T7.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The best move is to discard T7 as it is a single tile not part of any current meld and reduces the risk of others completing their hands.\nReason: Discarding T7 maintains existing chows (T3-4-5) and avoids giving others a tile needed for their sequences. Keeping F4 (seat wind) and potential dragon pungs (J2/J3) is strategic for scoring.\nAnswer: Play T7"}
