ard B3: This is part of the B suit, but without B2 or B4, it's hard to form a Chow.\n\n- Discard F4: This is a single South Wind tile. If the user wants to form a Pung of South Wind, they need two more. But with only one, discarding might be okay.\n\n- Discard B1: This is a terminal tile. If the user is going for All Terminals, they might want to keep it, but they don't have enough other terminals.\n\nSo the safest discard might be a tile that's not part of any potential meld. Looking at the hand, the J2 and J3 are single honor tiles. The user has only one each. If they discard J2 or J3, it's a safe play because they can't form a Pung yet. Similarly, F4 is a single tile. Discarding J2 or J3 might be better than keeping them as they can't contribute to melds yet.\n\nAlternatively, if the user can form a pair in the honor tiles, that might help. But with only one each, it's not possible. So discarding one of the honor tiles (J2 or J3) might be the best move.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, the user can play J2, J3, etc. The user has J2 and J3 in hand. The game history shows that the player sitting across has played J2 and J3. So maybe those tiles are less likely to be needed by others, but not sure.\n\nAnother angle: if the user can form a concealed hand (all self-drawn melds), that would give 4 points for Fully Concealed Hand. But since they already have a Chi (Chow) of W3-W4-W5, that's an exposed meld, so the hand is not fully concealed. Therefore, the user can't get the Fully Concealed Hand points. So maybe it's better to focus on other patterns.\n\nLooking at the melds, the user has a Chow in the Character suit. If they can form another Chow in the T suit (T3-T4-T5), that would give two Chows. Then they need two more melds. The B tiles are scattered, but maybe they can form a pair with B5 and B6? No, a pair is two of the same. So B5 and B6 can't be a pair. The B1, B3, B5, B6 could form a Pung if they have three of the same, but they don't. So maybe the user needs to form a pair in the honor tiles.\n\nThe user has F4, J2, J3. If they can form a pair of J2 or J3, that would help. But they only have one each. So maybe they need to discard one of the honor tiles to focus on other parts.\n\nAlternatively, the user could aim for a Seven Pairs hand. But with the current tiles, they have T4 (two), and others are single. Not enough for seven pairs.\n\nAnother possibility: the user could try to form a Pure Straight in the T suit. For example, T3-T4-T5 as a Chow, and then maybe another Chow with T4-T5-T6, but they don't have T6. So that's not possible.\n\nLooking back, the best option seems to be discarding a tile that's not part of any potential meld. The J2 or J3 are single honor tiles. Discarding J2 might be better if the player sitting across has already played J2, reducing the chance of others needing it. Alternatively, J3 was played by the player sitting across, so maybe J3 is less needed.\n\nAlternatively, the user could discard B1, a terminal tile, but it's not part of any possible meld.\n\nWait, the user has F4 (South Wind). The prevalent wind is South Wind. If they can form a Pung of South Wind, that would give 2 points for Prevalent Wind. But they only have one F4. So not possible yet.\n\nConsidering all this, the safest discard is probably J2 or J3. Let's check the valid actions. The user can play J2, J3, etc. Between J2 and J3, which is better to discard? The game history shows the player across has played J2 and J3. So maybe discarding J2 is safer if the player across has already played it, but it's a minor consideration.\n\nAnother angle: if the user keeps J2 and J3, they might be able to form a pair later. But since they need a pair in the final hand, having two of the same is necessary. So if the user can form a pair of J2 or J3, that would be good. But with only one each, they need to draw another. So maybe it's better to keep both and try to draw another. But if they discard one, they can focus on other parts.\n\nIn this case, the user has J2 and J3. If they discard J2, they can keep J3 for a potential pair. Alternatively, if they discard J3, they keep J2. Since the user's seat wind is West (F3), and they have F4 (South Wind), maybe keeping J2 (Green Dragon) is more useful for a potential Dragon Pung. But with only one, it's not helpful.\n\nGiven that, perhaps the best move is to discard J2 or J3. Between the two, J2 is Green Dragon, which is part of the Three Dragons. If the user can form a Pung of J2 (Green Dragon), that would be good. But since they have only one, it's not possible yet. Similarly for J3 (White Dragon).\n\nAlternatively, the user could discard B1, which is a terminal tile not part of any meld. But if they're going for All Terminals, they need more, which they don't have.\n\nSo the most logical choice is to discard J2 or J3. Let's check the valid actions. The user can play J2. So the answer would be Play J2.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The best move is to discard J2, as it is a single honor tile with limited potential for forming high-value melds in the current hand. Keeping other tiles like T4 and T3 allows for potential Chows in the T suit, while discarding J2 avoids wasting a tile that cannot contribute to immediate melds or pairs.\n\nReason: Discarding J2 removes a tile that cannot form a Pung or pair immediately, freeing up space to focus on forming Chows in the T suit (e.g., T3-T4-T5) and other melds. This maintains flexibility for higher-scoring patterns like Pure Straight or All Chows.\n\nAnswer: Play J2"}
