maybe they don't need it. So discarding T6 might be safe.\n\nLooking at the possible patterns, if the user can form a hand with four chows and a pair, that would be a Pure Straight (16 points) or something similar. The existing two chows are W3-4-5 and T3-4-5. If they can form two more chows, that would be four chows. For example, T5-6-7 (but the next player has that), or B3-4-5 (if they have B3, B4, B5). The user has B3 and two B5s. If they can get B4, they could form B3-4-5. But B4 is available in the unshown tiles (B4:3). So if the user can get B4, they can form a chow. Similarly, if they have T6, they can form T5-6-7 if they get T7, but the next player already has that.\n\nSo, the user's current hand could be building towards four chows. If they can form a B3-4-5 chow and a T5-6-7 chow, that would be four chows. But they need the B4 and T7. Since T7 is already in the next player's meld, maybe they can't get it. So the B3-4-5 is more feasible.\n\nTo form B3-4-5, the user needs B4. The unshown B4 count is 3. If the user discards B3, they might be giving up the chance to form that chow. So keeping B3 is better.\n\nSimilarly, the user has T4 and T5. If they can get T6, they can form T4-5-6. But they already have T6 in their hand. So if they have T4, T5, T6, they can form a chow. But the user already has a T3-4-5 chow. So having T4-5-6 would be another chow. That would make three chows (W3-4-5, T3-4-5, T4-5-6). Then they need one more chow and a pair.\n\nBut the user's hand has T4, T5, T6. So if they keep those, they can form T4-5-6. So maybe discard a tile that's not part of that chow. The T9 is not part of any chow, so discarding T9 might be a good option.\n\nAlternatively, if they discard W6, they can focus on forming the B3-4-5 and T4-5-6 chows.\n\nSo, the possible best move is to discard T9 or W6. Between those, discarding W6 might be better because it's not part of any potential chow, whereas T9 could be part of a pair for All Terminals or Seven Pairs.\n\nBut the user needs to form a winning hand with at least 8 points. If they can form four chows (16 points), that's good. To get four chows, they need the B3-4-5 and T4-5-6. So keeping T4, T5, T6 is important. Discarding T9 would allow them to focus on those chows.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to discard T9. That way, they can work on forming the necessary chows for a Pure Straight or similar pattern.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The optimal move is to discard T9 to focus on forming potential chows in the B and T suits for a high-point pattern like Pure Straight.\nReason: Discarding T9 removes a terminal tile that doesn't contribute to the existing chows in W and T suits. Keeping B3 and T4/T5/T6 supports building additional chows (B3-4-5 and T4-5-6), which are critical for a Pure Straight (16 points). The T9 is less valuable for this strategy compared to preserving tiles that can form sequential melds.\nAnswer: Play T9"}
