 and the player across has a W2-W3-W4 chow. \n\nIf the user plays T3, the player across might have a Chi if they need T2 or T4. But the player across has already made a Chi W3, so maybe they have a meld in W. Not sure. \n\nAlternatively, playing W7 is a high tile, maybe less likely to be needed for a Chi. \n\nBetween the options, playing W7 might be the safest because it's a single tile and there are three copies left. Playing T3 or T4 might be part of a potential Chi for someone else. \n\nSo the best move is to play W7. That way, the user discards a tile that's part of a possible future chow but doesn't risk giving others a chance to form a meld. Also, keeping T3 and T4 in hand might help if they can form another meld later. \n\nTherefore, the answer should be Play W7.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The best move is to discard W7 to avoid potential conflicts and maintain flexibility for forming additional melds.\nReason: Playing W7 is a high-character tile with three remaining copies, making it less likely to be needed for others' melds. Keeping T3 and T4 supports potential future chows in the Bamboo suit, while W3/W4 are part of an existing meld. This move balances safety and strategic hand development.\nAnswer: Play W7"}
